00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:30.830
Brex is accepting community contributions to crab trap for single sign on role-based access control

00:08:30.830 --> 00:08:37.670
and escalation workflows. Okay, let's pop the hood on agent identity. Everyone treats agent

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:44.490
identity as a simple give each agent its own key check box. In practice it is a three layer enforcement

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:49.870
problem that most production systems still solve with borrowed human or service account credentials.

00:08:50.450 --> 00:08:57.130
The first layer is issuance creating a scoped revocable identity at agent spawn time instead of

00:08:57.130 --> 00:09:04.630
reusing a shared API key. The second layer is runtime mediation. Every outbound call must carry that

00:09:04.630 --> 00:09:09.970
identity so policy engines can evaluate it before the request reaches the target API.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:17.270
The third layer is attribution logging which identity performed which actions so post incident forensics

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:23.770
can isolate the blast radius. When these layers are missing a single compromised agent can act with

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:30.610
the full permissions of every other agent sharing its credential. That pattern appears in 69% of surveyed

00:09:30.610 --> 00:09:37.490
enterprises. The practical trade-off is latency versus safety. Adding a network proxy or sidecar

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:43.650
for identity enforcement adds measurable overhead but collapses the blast radius from entire fleet

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:50.590
to single agent. Most teams discover the gap only after the first incident. The fix that actually

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:56.510
moves the needle is treating non-human identity as infrastructure rather than an application level

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:02.650
afterthought. So when should you actually reach for this versus the alternative? Tomorrow keep an

